M.R2[0] = (f"Four checks bound what the within-city estimates can claim (Fig. 2). First, places that were later sealed were already warmer: in cells unsealed in 1985–1989, the 1985–1989 heat island was associated with later impervious cover ({Pin('nco')}), a negative-control outcome that later sealing cannot have caused[@lipsitch2010]. Adding the 1985–1989 heat island as a confounder lowered the total effect to {m(R3['lag_all']['mu'])}–{m(R3['lag_unsealed']['mu'])} °C (Fig. 2a). This adjustment may over-control, because land is often cleared before it is sealed, so part of the treatment can already be in the 1985–1989 heat island. In cells that were still vegetated in 1985–1989 (NDVI above the city's rural median; {R5['lagveg']['k']} city-regions), where clearing had not yet occurred, the adjustment changed the effect only from {m(R5['lagveg_ref']['mu'])} to {m(R5['lagveg']['mu'])} °C. The lagged estimates are therefore lower bounds rather than corrections.")
M.R2[2] = (f"Third, warming was concentrated when land was converted. Because back-cast dating shares the Landsat spectra with surface temperature and ESA CCI dating is coarse (300 m), we required both products to agree. Cells converted between 1998–2002 and 2012–2016 warmed by {P('cA_post')} per ten percentage points during conversion, {PM('cA_pre1')} °C before and {PM('cA_post2')} °C after ({R3['cA_pre1']['k']} city-regions; Fig. 2b). The pre-conversion warming is not zero, so parallel trends hold only approximately; net of it, {m(AD('cA')['mu'])} °C (95% CI {rng(AD('cA')['lo'], AD('cA')['hi'])} °C) remained. Measuring the dose by the CCI change instead, so that dating and dose are both independent of Landsat, gave {m(R5['cc_post']['mu'])} °C during and {m(R5['cc_pre1']['mu'])} °C before conversion per ten percentage points of CCI urban fraction (net {m(R5['cc_net']['mu'])} °C; 95% CI {rng(R5['cc_net']['lo'], R5['cc_net']['hi'])} °C), a smaller effect in a coarser unit but the same pattern. The slight cooling after conversion is consistent with partial recovery of vegetation or with dating error, and too few conversions after 2014 were dated by both products to test[@roth2023,rambachan2023].")

M.R4[2] = (f"Screening needs the right order more than the right number. Sorted into thirds by their own total effect, the causal transfer model placed {pct(DEC['causal_all']['tercile_agree'][0])} (95% CI {pct(DEC['causal_all']['tercile_agree'][1])}–{pct(DEC['causal_all']['tercile_agree'][2])}) of cities correctly, against {pct(DEC['full_all']['tercile_agree'][0])} for the full model and {pct(DEC['zone_baseline']['tercile_agree'][0])} for a baseline that assigns each city the median effect of its climate zone (Table 1). "
           f"The cities' own estimates share the identification assumptions of the causal model, so we also used a benchmark that is partly independent of them: the warming observed at conversions dated by both land-cover products, net of pre-trends. Against it, the causal model again ranked cities best (Spearman correlation {B(XT['causal_all']['rho'])}, against {m(XT['full_all']['rho'][0])} for the full model, {m(XT['zone_baseline']['rho'][0])} for the zone baseline and {m(XT['minimal_all']['rho'][0])} for the minimal model). This benchmark is itself a DML estimate; with the dose measured by ESA CCI rather than by the back-cast, the order was the same ({m(XC['causal_all']['rho'][0])}, {m(XC['full_all']['rho'][0])}, {m(XC['zone_baseline']['rho'][0])} and {m(XC['minimal_all']['rho'][0])}). "
           f"The {R5['sel_n']} benchmark cities are those with enough dated conversions; their own effects resembled those of the other cities ({m(R5['sel_pool_in']['mu'])} against {m(R5['sel_pool_out']['mu'])} °C), although arid and continental cities are over-represented.")
M.R4.append(f"The ranking advantage holds on average, not everywhere. The causal model ordered cities better than the full model in {['no', 'one', 'two', 'three', 'four', 'five', 'six', 'all'][n_causal_better]} of the seven world regions, whereas the full model did better in {', '.join(full_better[:-1]) + ' and ' + full_better[-1] if len(full_better) > 1 else full_better[0]}. In Europe, for example, the causal model was closer in size (mean absolute difference {m(R4['eu_mae']['causal_all'])} against {m(R4['eu_mae']['full_all'])} °C) but ordered cities less well ({m(R4['eu_rho']['causal_all'])} against {m(R4['eu_rho']['full_all'])}). City-level 90% intervals for transferred effects (half-width {m(S['city_interval']['halfwidth'])} °C) are as wide as the spread between cities; transferred effects order cities, but they do not replace a local estimate.")

